debian/project - board/version specific customizations
=====================================================

debian/overlay/ is copied into the rootfs of *every* board, so it must only
hold content that is valid everywhere (sleep targets, GNOME power defaults,
generic services, ...).  Anything that belongs to one board, one panel or one
product version goes here instead, in its own directory:

    debian/project/<project>/<same layout as the rootfs>

For example, the RK3572 RP08B V10 board keeps its LED wiring and its screen
layout there:

    debian/project/hugsun_rk3572_rp08b_v10/etc/default/led-ctl
    debian/project/hugsun_rk3572_rp08b_v10/etc/xdg/autostart/zz-hdmi-primary.desktop
    debian/project/hugsun_rk3572_rp08b_v10/usr/bin/hdmi-primary

How the right directory is selected
-----------------------------------

The directory name is resolved from the current build config, first hit wins:

    1. $RK_PROJECT                     explicit (Config.in.overlay option)
    2. $RK_DEFCONFIG minus "_defconfig"  e.g. hugsun_rk3572_rp08b_v10
    3. $RK_KERNEL_DTS_NAME               e.g. rk3572-hugsun-rp08b-v10

Because of the candidate order, a project directory normally carries the name
of the defconfig it was made for, and several defconfigs may share one project
directory by setting RK_PROJECT to the same value.  A build whose board has no
directory here simply gets nothing installed (a notice is logged).

When it is installed
--------------------

device/rockchip/common/post-hooks/92-project.sh, during the post-rootfs stage,
for debian rootfs only.  It runs after the SDK overlays (90-overlay.sh), so a
project file overrides an overlay file, and before 97-ldcache.sh / 99-owner.sh,
so those still see the installed files (ldconfig cache, ownership).

Contents are copied with the same policy as SDK overlays (permissions
normalized, uid/gid fixed up by 99-owner.sh).  A project may ship an
install.sh that is executed instead of the copy; it is called with

    install.sh <target rootfs dir> <os name>

Adding a project
----------------

    mkdir -p debian/project/<name>/
    # copy files in, mirroring the rootfs layout
    ./build.sh          # or ./build.sh rootfs

To check what got installed, look for the hook output in the build log:

    output/log/latest/post-rootfs.log   # "Installing project: <name> ..."

Board specific *runtime* decisions
----------------------------------

A file installed for one board can still be harmless on another one if it
gates itself at runtime on the device tree, the way usr/bin/led-ctl does with
the COMPATIBLE= key of /etc/default/led-ctl (compared against
/proc/device-tree/compatible).  Prefer the build time project directory for
content that is clearly board specific, and keep such a runtime gate as a
second line of defence.

Screen layout is deliberately *not* shipped as ~/.config/monitors.xml: mutter
requires connector+vendor+product+serial in every <monitorspec> and matches all
four exactly, so a stored layout is pinned to one specific monitor (and the
file is rejected as a whole when a field is missing).  Use a script that talks
to org.gnome.Mutter.DisplayConfig and addresses outputs by connector name
instead - see usr/bin/hdmi-primary.

Two things measured on RK3572 RP08B that are easy to get wrong:

  * gdctl must run as the session user.  As root "gdctl show" and
    "gdctl set" exit 1 without printing anything, so a layout that
    "silently does nothing" is usually a wrong uid, not a wrong
    connector name.  The autostart entry takes care of this.

  * the connector name mutter uses is not always the kernel one: sysfs
    exposes card0-HDMI-A-1 while gdctl reports that output as HDMI-1.
    usr/bin/hdmi-primary therefore reads the names back from
    "gdctl show" (LC_ALL=C, the output is parsed) instead of hardcoding
    either spelling.

    $ gdctl show | sed -n '/Logical monitors/,$p'     # after the script
    ├──Logical monitor #1 ... Primary: no   DSI-1  (Built-in display)
    └──Logical monitor #2 ... Primary: yes  HDMI-1 (Lenovo Group Limited 22")

